node_modules/
dist/
.env
.env.local
*.log

# Generated episodes. Every run writes its own directory of artifacts here -
# corpus, claims, script, renders. Deliberately NOT committed: it is large,
# it is reproducible from the inputs that ARE committed, and a rejected take
# is not something the history needs to carry forever.
runs/

# LIBRARY.md and library.json are rebuilt from the run directories by
# `foundry library`, and runs/ is ignored - so on any machine but the one that
# made them they would describe runs that are not there. Committing them would
# be committing a list of things nobody else can open.
LIBRARY.md
library.json

# series.json is deliberately NOT ignored either, for a sharper version of the
# same reason. It records which platform series each show publishes into, series
# creation has no create-or-get, and there is no way to look one up by title on
# the ingest credential. Lose it and the next publish makes a SECOND shelf that
# starts again at episode one, while the first keeps every episode already
# published. Nothing about either looks wrong in isolation.

# bibles/ is deliberately NOT ignored, and it is the one generated thing here
# that is committed. A run is reproducible from its inputs; a series bible is
# not - it IS the series' memory, and losing it means every later episode
# contradicts freely with nothing to notice. It is a few kilobytes, it changes
# once per published episode, and a diff on it is the most readable record of
# what a show has established that exists anywhere.

# seasons/ is NOT ignored, for the same reason bibles/ is not. A bible is the
# series' memory and a season plan is its intention: hand-edited, referenced by
# every episode written against it, and not reproducible from anything. Losing
# one orphans every episode that was written to it.

# catalogue.json is NOT ignored either. It records which subjects have already
# been made, and runs/ - the only other place that knows - is ignored, so on a
# fresh clone the duplicate check would believe the studio had made nothing.

# Scratch audio
*.wav
*.mp3

# The beat library. The RECIPES in beds/*.json are committed and are a few
# hundred bytes each; the rendered loops beside them are not, and are covered by
# the *.mp3 line above. Synthesis is deterministic, so a recipe rebuilds its
# exact audio on any machine - which is the same bargain runs/ makes, and the
# reason a quarter of a megabyte per beat never enters the history.
!beds/*.json

# The built interface. Rebuilt with `npm --prefix web run build`.
web/dist/
web/node_modules/
# TypeScript's incremental build cache. It was tracked for a while, so every
# build produced a diff nobody could read and nobody wanted.
web/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo

# Real credentials for real accounts on production. See src/publish/account.ts.
# This repo has no remote, and this line is what keeps it that way if it ever gets one.
accounts.json

# Generated channel artwork. Rebuilt by `channel-setup`.
art/

# The cross-process release lock. Runtime state, never committed.
.release.lock
release.log
release.log.old

# Your own background tracks, uploaded in the studio. Not the repo's to keep.
music/
